// Generated sourced history; see generate_crew_presentation_catalog.py.
    {"bounty-hunter", "Bounty Hunter — Fictional composite", "Express companies offered rewards for criminals.\nWells Fargo agents worked with sheriffs.\nTheir role differed from statutory arrest powers.", "Wells Fargo Corporate Archives — Outwitting outlaws", "https://history.wf.com/outwitting-outlaws/", "1870s–1880s · Western United States\n\nFull historical note:\nWestern express companies offered rewards and enlisted citizens to help identify criminals. Wells Fargo’s special agents also worked with sheriffs; the company’s account distinguishes their investigative work from statutory arrest powers.\n\nWells Fargo Corporate Archives — Outwitting outlaws\nhttps://history.wf.com/outwitting-outlaws/\nDocuments nineteenth-century armed messengers, special agents and reward posters; private special agents lacked independent arrest powers."},
    {"tom-horn", "Tom Horn — Historical person", "Horn was a freighter, cowboy and army scout.\nHe joined Pinkerton around 1890, then worked\nfor Wyoming cattle interests in the 1890s.", "Wyoming State Historical Society: Tom Horn Artifacts at the Wyoming State Museum", "https://wyohistory.org/blog/tom-horn-artifacts-wyoming-state-museum", "About 1895 · Wyoming\n\nFull historical note:\nTom Horn worked as a freighter, cowboy and army scout before detective work. He joined the Pinkerton agency around 1890 and later worked for Wyoming cattle interests; the figure represents that later period.\n\nWyoming State Historical Society: Tom Horn Artifacts at the Wyoming State Museum\nhttps://wyohistory.org/blog/tom-horn-artifacts-wyoming-state-museum\nEarlier occupations, 1880s army service, Pinkerton work circa 1890 and Wyoming stock-detective employment from the 1890s.\n\nNPS: Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nPeriod sack/frock coats and waistcoats; long high-necked dresses. General clothing context, not this exact costume."},
    {"hellfire-preacher", "Hellfire Preacher — Fictional composite", "Protestant ministers founded Western churches.\nSheldon Jackson's papers document mission work.\nThis preacher's violent card ability is fiction.", "Presbyterian Historical Society: Sheldon Jackson Papers", "https://pcusa.org/historical-society/collections/research-tools/guides-archival-collections/rg-239", "1870s–1880s · Western United States Protestant congregations\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional preacher draws on Protestant ministers who established congregations across the nineteenth-century West. Sheldon Jackson’s surviving papers document western missionary work; the card’s violent ability is fiction.\n\nPresbyterian Historical Society: Sheldon Jackson Papers\nhttps://pcusa.org/historical-society/collections/research-tools/guides-archival-collections/rg-239\nWestern missionary correspondence and photographs spanning the relevant decades, including Colorado, Dakota, Montana and Utah."},
    {"revival-sister", "Revival Sister — Fictional composite", "Seven women joined the Salvation Army's official\nU.S. mission in 1880; it reached California in 1883.\nThis organizer reflects women's religious work.", "Salvation Army: An Enduring Commitment", "https://ctri.salvationarmy.org/usn/news/an-enduring-commitment", "1883–1890s · Western United States, with documented California precedent\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional revival organizer reflects documented women’s religious leadership. Seven women accompanied the Salvation Army’s official US mission in 1880, and its work reached California in 1883.\n\nSalvation Army: An Enduring Commitment\nhttps://ctri.salvationarmy.org/usn/news/an-enduring-commitment\nSeven women and one man arrived in New York on March 10, 1880.\n\nSalvation Army: History\nhttps://southernusa.salvationarmy.org/santaclara/news/history-of-the-salvation-army\n1883 California expansion.\n\nNPS: Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nPeriod sack/frock coats and waistcoats; long high-necked dresses. General clothing context, not this exact costume."},
    {"pinkerton-agent", "Pinkerton Agent — Fictional composite", "Pinkerton agents investigated robberies and\nworked for railroads and other private clients.\nSome work involved labor disputes and spying.", "Library of Congress: Pinkerton’s National Detective Agency Records", "https://findingaids.loc.gov/repositories/19/resources/2451", "1870s–1880s · United States private detective work, including western railroad and outlaw investigations\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional detective draws on Pinkerton’s National Detective Agency. Its agents investigated robberies and worked for railroads and other clients; some assignments involved labor disputes and controversial undercover work.\n\nLibrary of Congress: Pinkerton’s National Detective Agency Records\nhttps://findingaids.loc.gov/repositories/19/resources/2451\nSurviving agency investigation, financial and personnel records.\n\nLibrary of Congress: Pinkertons, Topics in Chronicling America\nhttps://guides.loc.gov/chronicling-america-pinkertons\nDated events including McParlan’s undercover investigation and the 1877 executions."},
    {"lozen", "Lozen — Historical person", "Lozen, a Chiricahua Apache warrior, was\nVictorio's sister. After his death she resisted,\nthen surrendered with Geronimo's band in 1886.", "Mescalero Apache Tribe: Our Culture", "https://mescaleroapachetribe.com/our-culture/", "1880s · Chiricahua Apache homelands, present-day southwestern United States and northern Mexico\n\nFull historical note:\nLozen was a Chiricahua Apache warrior and the sister of Victorio. She continued with Apache resistance after his death and surrendered with Geronimo’s remaining band in 1886.\n\nMescalero Apache Tribe: Our Culture\nhttps://mescaleroapachetribe.com/our-culture/\nTribal account identifies Lozen’s nation, warrior role, relationship to Victorio and 1886 surrender.\n\nNational Archives: Geronimo and fellow Apache prisoners, 1886\nhttps://catalog.archives.gov/id/530797\nExisting project’s period photograph reference for Apache clothing; photographic interpretation is not tribal approval."},
    {"regulator", "Regulator — Fictional composite", "After Tunstall's murder in 1878, his followers\nand McSween's became the Regulators.\nRival factions escalated the Lincoln County War.", "New Mexico Historic Sites: Apache and Lincoln County Wars", "https://nmhistoricsites.org/fort-stanton/the-apache-and-lincoln-county-wars-1870-1896", "1878 · Lincoln County, New Mexico Territory\n\nFull historical note:\nIn 1878, followers of John Tunstall and Alexander McSween became the Regulators after Tunstall’s murder. Rival factions claimed legal authority while escalating the Lincoln County War.\n\nNew Mexico Historic Sites: Apache and Lincoln County Wars\nhttps://nmhistoricsites.org/fort-stanton/the-apache-and-lincoln-county-wars-1870-1896\n1878 Regulator faction, deputization and opposing commercial interests.\n\nNPS: Cowboy Gear\nhttps://www.nps.gov/pisp/learn/photosmultimedia/cowboy-gear.htm\nFunctional felt hats, kerchiefs, leather riding equipment; individual colors and cuts remain reconstruction."},
    {"billy-the-kid", "Billy the Kid — Historical person", "Billy the Kid died in New Mexico in 1881.\nCourt, government and newspaper records survive.\nThese outweigh later screen portrayals and myths.", "New Mexico State Records Center & Archives — Billy the Kid Research Resources", "https://www.srca.nm.gov/billy-the-kid/", "circa 1879–1881 · New Mexico Territory\n\nFull historical note:\nBilly the Kid’s history survives in territorial government, court and newspaper records. He died in 1881; those records should take precedence over later screen portrayals and claims that every newly discovered photograph shows him.\n\nNew Mexico State Records Center & Archives — Billy the Kid Research Resources\nhttps://www.srca.nm.gov/billy-the-kid/\nInstitutional guide to territorial records, newspapers and death in 1881.\n\nNew Mexico Tourism Department — Billy the Kid in New Mexico\nhttps://www.newmexico.org/things-to-do/arts-culture/historical/billy-the-kid/\nLincoln courthouse escape context and death at Fort Sumner on July 14, 1881."},
    {"gatling-gunner", "Gatling Gunner — Fictional composite", "This fictional gunner depicts a frontier crewman.\nPeriod Gatling guns used wheeled field carriages.\nHand cranking drove their rotating barrel cluster.", "Gatling Gun Battery. Fort Lincoln., Frank Jay Haynes, ca. 1880", "https://www.cartermuseum.org/collection/gatling-gun-battery-fort-lincoln-p197624141", "1874–1880 · U.S. Army frontier garrison, Dakota Territory\n\nFull historical note:\nFictional composite of a late-1870s U.S. Army gun crew member. Period illustrations show Gatling guns carried on large wheeled field carriages; hand cranking drove their rotating barrel cluster.\n\nGatling Gun Battery. Fort Lincoln., Frank Jay Haynes, ca. 1880\nhttps://www.cartermuseum.org/collection/gatling-gun-battery-fort-lincoln-p197624141\nPrimary photograph and museum dating establish the frontier military crew context; military short coats, forage caps, light trousers. Not used to identify the gun model; not a named sitter portrait.\n\nMontana Historical Society, Chapter 7 primary photographs\nhttps://mths.mt.gov/education/StoriesOfTheLand/Part2/Chapter7/Ch7Educators\nHaynes Fort Lincoln battery photograph H-61, catalogued June 1877, visually inspected for enlisted crew clothing, caps and cartridge belts. Catalog dates differ; broad late-1870s reconstruction disclosed.\n\nScientific American, February 7, 1874, engraving reproduced on ASAC Bulletin 65 page 35\nhttps://americansocietyofarmscollectors.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/06/1991-B65-The-Development-of-the-Gatling-Gun.pdf\nPrimary period engraving on final PDF page documents Model 1874 Gatling on large two-wheel wooden field carriage with iron tires, axle, long trailing support, elevation screw and hand crank; feed drum removed. This exact configuration supplies the weapon silhouette.\n\nSmithsonian Libraries: Trade catalogs from Gatling Gun\nhttps://www.si.edu/object/trade-catalogs-gatling-gun%3ASILNMAHTL_10229\nCatalog record documents 1874 Gatling instructions and 1878 trade literature, confirming period manufacturing context; not used as a substitute for the inspected engraving.\n\nU.S. Army: Survey of Uniforms, Weapons and Accoutrements, p.30\nhttps://history.army.mil/html/museums/uniforms/survey_uwa.pdf\nPattern1874 sack coat: dark-blue wool, five brass front buttons; western Army clothing variation in the1870s. Supports clothing context, not an exact portrait or unit identification.\n\nSmithsonian NMAH: Model 1874 fatigue blouse, made 1877\nhttps://americanhistory.si.edu/collections/object/nmah_448644\nSurviving Army enlisted fatigue blouse establishes dark-blue wool and brass buttons in the intended period; exact branch trim is not copied because figure has no unit identity."},
    {"coach-guard", "Coach Guard — Fictional composite", "Express messengers guarded stagecoach valuables.\nThey commonly rode beside the driver.\nGuarding cargo distinguished their work.", "Wells Fargo Corporate Archives — Outwitting outlaws", "https://history.wf.com/outwitting-outlaws/", "1890–1899 · Western United States express stage routes\n\nFull historical note:\nExpress messengers guarded valuable shipments carried by stagecoach. They commonly rode beside the driver; the guarded cargo, such as a treasure box, distinguished this work from simply being a passenger carrying a gun.\n\nWells Fargo Corporate Archives — Outwitting outlaws\nhttps://history.wf.com/outwitting-outlaws/\nDocuments nineteenth-century armed messengers, special agents and reward posters; private special agents lacked independent arrest powers.\n\nSmithsonian National Museum of American History — Remington Model 1889 Double Barrel Break Action Shotgun\nhttps://www.si.edu/object/remington-arms-co-model-1889-double-barrel-break-action-shotgun%3Anmah_1119582\nDated surviving 1889 12-gauge break-action example, metal and wood, overall 87.8 cm and barrels 45.5 cm. This specific object anchors proportions, not proof that every messenger carried it.\n\nJohn Boessenecker — Shotguns and Stagecoaches, publisher excerpt\nhttps://us.macmillan.com/books/9781250184900/shotgunsandstagecoaches/\nSpecialist secondary history describes messengers and period double-barrel shotguns; supports role and armament, not current sling.\n\nRemington Society of America — Remington Museum Displays\nhttps://remingtonsociety.org/remington-museum-displays/\nModel 1889 was an outside-hammer double gun manufactured by the chosen 1890s period.\n\nUniversity of Rhode Island — Man’s Waistcoat, c.1890\nhttps://uritextilecollection.omeka.net/items/show/541\nDated surviving black double-breasted waistcoat, brown cotton back, three pairs of buttons, low shawl collar and welt pockets. Clothing-category reference, not proof of a messenger uniform.\n\nFashion Institute of Technology — 1890–1899\nhttps://fashionhistory.fitnyc.edu/1890-1899/\nPeriod menswear tailoring, waistcoats, trousers and civilian ensembles; institutional dress history. Plain outdoor clothing adaptation is stated reconstruction."},
    {"widow-with-a-shotgun", "Widow with a Shotgun — Fictional composite", "The Homestead Act of 1862 let eligible women\nclaim land and protected a claimant's widow.\nFrontier landholding was not exclusively male.", "NPS: Homestead Act of 1862, statutory text", "https://www.nps.gov/home/homestead-act-of-1862.htm", "About 1880 · Western United States homestead context\n\nFull historical note:\nThis is a fictional widowed homesteader. The Homestead Act of 1862 allowed eligible women to claim land and protected a widow’s rights when a claimant died; frontier landholding was not exclusively male.\n\nNPS: Homestead Act of 1862, statutory text\nhttps://www.nps.gov/home/homestead-act-of-1862.htm\nEligibility language and provisions for a widow/heirs; supports female homesteading, not a specific armed episode.\n\nNPS: Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nPeriod sack/frock coats and waistcoats; long high-necked dresses. General clothing context, not this exact costume."},
    {"homestead-defender", "Homestead Defender — Fictional composite", "Hispano families settled Colorado's San Luis Valley.\nThe Trujillo homesteads preserve their ranching\nhistory and adobe and log-building traditions.", "NPS: Trujillo Homesteads", "https://home.nps.gov/grsa/learn/historyculture/trujillo-homesteads.htm", "1860s–1880s · San Luis Valley, Colorado\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional homesteader reflects Hispano families who settled and ranched in Colorado’s San Luis Valley. The Trujillo homesteads preserve evidence of those families and their differing adobe and log-building traditions.\n\nNPS: Trujillo Homesteads\nhttps://home.nps.gov/grsa/learn/historyculture/trujillo-homesteads.htm\nHispanic settlement and ranching near the dunes beginning in the 1860s; Trujillo family history and buildings."},
    {"claim-jumper", "Claim Jumper — Fictional composite", "The Mining Law of 1872 formalized mineral claims.\nDiscovery, location and surveys mattered legally.\nA paper or pistol does not prove claim jumping.", "U.S. Government Publishing Office — Mining Law of 1872", "https://www.govinfo.gov/app/details/COMPS-5337", "1870s–1890s · Western mining districts\n\nFull historical note:\nThe Mining Law of 1872 formalized acquisition of mineral claims on eligible public land. Claim location, discovery and surveys had legal significance; a paper, ore bag or pistol does not by itself identify a person as a claim jumper.\n\nU.S. Government Publishing Office — Mining Law of 1872\nhttps://www.govinfo.gov/app/details/COMPS-5337\nOfficial compilation of the 1872 act; used only for historical context, not present legal guidance.\n\nBureau of Land Management — Locatable Minerals\nhttps://www.blm.gov/programs/energy-and-minerals/mining-and-minerals/locatable-minerals\nHistorical origin of the federal claim system in the Mining Law of 1872; occupation and location context only.\n\nNational Park Service — Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nThe sack-style coat and layered civilian ensemble fit documented garment categories, not an occupational uniform."},
    {"jesse-james", "Jesse James — Historical person", "James was a former Confederate guerrilla who\nrobbed banks, trains and stagecoaches.\nHis crimes harmed civilians despite heroic myths.", "State Historical Society of Missouri: Jesse James", "https://historicmissourians.shsmo.org/jesse-james/", "Late 1870s · Missouri and the central United States\n\nFull historical note:\nJesse James was a former Confederate guerrilla who later robbed banks, stagecoaches and trains. Contemporary publicity helped create a Robin Hood legend, but his crimes injured and killed civilians.\n\nState Historical Society of Missouri: Jesse James\nhttps://historicmissourians.shsmo.org/jesse-james/\nDated portraits, outlaw activity, train robberies beginning in 1873, and the distinction between crimes and later legend."},
    {"con-man", "Con Man — Fictional composite", "Soapy Smith's gang operated in Skagway during\nthe Klondike rush. In 1898 he used the building\nnow preserved as Jeff. Smiths Parlor.", "National Park Service — Jeff. Smiths Parlor Museum", "https://www.nps.gov/klgo/learn/historyculture/jeffsmithsparlor.htm", "1880s–1890s · Western United States; Skagway 1898 as documented comparison\n\nFull historical note:\nJefferson “Soapy” Smith and his associates operated in Skagway during the Klondike gold rush. In 1898 he occupied the building now preserved as Jeff. Smiths Parlor; this provides a documented comparison for a fictional Western confidence man.\n\nNational Park Service — Jeff. Smiths Parlor Museum\nhttps://www.nps.gov/klgo/learn/historyculture/jeffsmithsparlor.htm\nSoapy Smith’s associates and occupation of the building beginning May 1898.\n\nNational Park Service — The Many Faces of Jeff. Smiths Parlor\nhttps://home.nps.gov/articles/klgo-khns-soapys-parlor.htm\nArrival in Skagway in 1897, parlor in 1898, and caution that the building’s later museum history differs from Smith’s brief occupancy.\n\nNational Park Service — Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nFrock coats and bowler hats are documented civilian menswear categories; neither signifies criminal occupation.\n\nMetropolitan Museum of Art — Waistcoat, late 1870s–early 1880s, 1979.34.1\nhttps://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/91135\nDated silk waistcoat supports shaped sleeveless waistcoat silhouette; its brocade, collar and exact closure differ from this mesh. Not a costume match."},
    {"bass-reeves", "Bass Reeves — Historical person", "Born enslaved, Reeves became a deputy in 1875.\nHe served in Indian Territory and Arkansas.\nPopular arrest and killing totals are disputed.", "National Park Service — Bass Reeves (Site Bulletin)", "https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/bass-reeves-site-bulletin.htm", "1880s · Indian Territory and Western District of Arkansas\n\nFull historical note:\nBorn enslaved around 1838, Bass Reeves entered deputy-marshal service in 1875. The National Park Service cautions that familiar totals of arrests and killings rest on an incomplete and exaggerated record, so those figures should not be presented as settled fact.\n\nNational Park Service — Bass Reeves (Site Bulletin)\nhttps://www.nps.gov/articles/000/bass-reeves-site-bulletin.htm\nIdentity, approximate birth date, deputy appointment and explicit cautions about legendary career statistics; includes historical portrait.\n\nNational Park Service — Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nSack-coat tailoring is documented period menswear; this does not identify Reeves’s actual coat."},
    {"bartender", "Bartender — Fictional composite", "Bartenders learned a wide range of mixed drinks.\nJerry Thomas's guide records period recipes\nand skilled professional drink preparation.", "Library of Congress — Jerry Thomas, The Bar-tender’s Guide, 1887 edition", "https://www.loc.gov/item/07000656/", "1870s–1890s · American saloon/bar context\n\nFull historical note:\nNineteenth-century bartenders worked from a substantial repertoire of mixed drinks. Jerry Thomas’s surviving bar-tender’s guide records recipes and professional drink preparation, showing that saloon service involved learned skills.\n\nLibrary of Congress — Jerry Thomas, The Bar-tender’s Guide, 1887 edition\nhttps://www.loc.gov/item/07000656/\nContemporary professional manual; this catalog copy is dated 1887, not the first 1862 edition.\n\nLibrary of Congress — American Mixology: Recipe Books from the Pre-Prohibition Era\nhttps://guides.loc.gov/early-mixology-books\nInstitutional guide to period bartender manuals, 1869–1911.\n\nNational Park Service — Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nPeriod civilian menswear included multi-piece suits and waistcoats; this does not establish a saloon uniform."},
    {"piano-player", "Piano Player — Fictional composite", "Blind Boone played with Black saloon musicians\nin 1870s Missouri. Around 1880 he began touring\nwith John Lange Jr. This pianist is fictional.", "State Historical Society of Missouri: “Blind” Boone", "https://historicmissourians.shsmo.org/blind-boone/", "1870s–1880s · Missouri and the central American West\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional pianist reflects a documented Black musical presence in nineteenth-century Missouri. John William “Blind” Boone played with Black saloon musicians in the 1870s and began his touring partnership with John Lange Jr. around 1880.\n\nState Historical Society of Missouri: “Blind” Boone\nhttps://historicmissourians.shsmo.org/blind-boone/\nExplicit 1870s Black saloon-musician context and December 1879 paid piano engagement; stronger early evidence than assigning Scott Joplin’s later career to 1880."},
    {"doc-holliday", "Doc Holliday — Historical person", "Doc Holliday trained as a dentist and moved west\nwith tuberculosis. He became a gambler and\njoined the Earps in the 1881 Tombstone gunfight.", "Kansas Historical Society: John Henry “Doc” Holliday", "https://www.kansasmemory.gov/item/218251", "1880–1887 · Tombstone, Arizona Territory, and the American West\n\nFull historical note:\nJohn Henry “Doc” Holliday trained as a dentist and moved west while suffering from tuberculosis. He became a gambler and joined the Earps in the 1881 Tombstone gunfight.\n\nKansas Historical Society: John Henry “Doc” Holliday\nhttps://www.kansasmemory.gov/item/218251\nPeriod portrait, dental graduation in 1872, move west, gambling and Tombstone associations."},
    {"laudanum-doctor", "Laudanum Doctor — Fictional composite", "Physicians used opium preparations for pain.\nIn the 1876 Little Bighorn campaign, Henry Porter\ntreated wounded men with laudanum and morphine.", "Smithsonian National Museum of American History: P LAUDANOP", "https://americanhistory.si.edu/collections/object/nmah_993563", "1870s–1880s · Western United States\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional doctor draws on a period when physicians used opium preparations for pain. During the 1876 Little Bighorn campaign, Doctor Henry Porter treated wounded soldiers with laudanum and morphine.\n\nSmithsonian National Museum of American History: P LAUDANOP\nhttps://americanhistory.si.edu/collections/object/nmah_993563\nMuseum pharmaceutical container and explanation of laudanum as an opium solution used for pain.\n\nNPS: Doctor Henry Porter\nhttps://home.nps.gov/libi/learn/historyculture/doctor-henry-porter.htm\nSpecific western 1876 physician context: Porter used laudanum and morphine for pain during the Little Bighorn campaign. The fictional civilian figure is not Porter.\n\nOregon Health & Science University: Leather medicine case with vials, 1880\nhttps://digitalcollections.ohsu.edu/record/4126\nSurviving 1880 leather medicine container and glass vials support period material/equipment context, not an exact match for this particular modeled bag."},
    {"barn-burner", "Barn Burner — Fictional composite", "In 1878, a Lincoln County War faction burned\nAlexander McSween's house in New Mexico.\nThis fictional arsonist is not a documented person.", "New Mexico Historic Sites — The Apache and Lincoln County Wars, 1870–1896", "https://nmhistoricsites.org/fort-stanton/the-apache-and-lincoln-county-wars-1870-1896", "1870s–1880s · Western United States; contextual example Lincoln, New Mexico\n\nFull historical note:\nDuring the Lincoln County War in 1878, members of the Murphy–Dolan faction set fire to Alexander McSween’s house. This documents destructive fire in a Western conflict; it does not establish a professional “barn burner” occupation or this character’s biography.\n\nNew Mexico Historic Sites — The Apache and Lincoln County Wars, 1870–1896\nhttps://nmhistoricsites.org/fort-stanton/the-apache-and-lincoln-county-wars-1870-1896\nInstitutional account of the burning of McSween’s house.\n\nLibrary of Congress — Lexington Intelligencer, July 17, 1880\nhttps://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ndnp/mohi/batch_mohi_eeyore_ver01/data/sn87052143/00294556552/1880071701/0577.pdf\nContemporary newspaper mentions a kerosene can; demonstrates period terminology but not the rendered can’s exact construction."},
    {"railroad-saboteur", "Railroad Saboteur — Fictional composite", "At Wilcox, Wyoming, in June 1899, robbers used\nexplosives on a train's express car.\nA major pursuit followed. This outlaw is fictional.", "Wyoming Historical Society: The Outlaw and His Lawyer", "https://www.wyohistory.org/encyclopedia/outlaw-and-his-lawyer-butch-cassidy-and-douglas-preston", "1899 · Wyoming, United States\n\nFull historical note:\nThis is a fictional rail outlaw, not a named robber. In June 1899, robbers at Wilcox, Wyoming, damaged a train’s express car with explosives; the incident brought a major pursuit.\n\nWyoming Historical Society: The Outlaw and His Lawyer\nhttps://www.wyohistory.org/encyclopedia/outlaw-and-his-lawyer-butch-cassidy-and-douglas-preston\nWilcox robbery on June 2, 1899 and destruction of the express car; contextualizes the fictional role, not a specific likeness.\n\nNPS: Chinese Labor and the Iron Road\nhttps://home.nps.gov/gosp/learn/historyculture/chinese-labor-and-the-iron-road.htm\nChinese construction workers and their June 1867 strike; does not support depicting a Chinese saboteur.\n\nNPS: Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nPeriod sack/frock coats and waistcoats; long high-necked dresses. General clothing context, not this exact costume.\n\nTICCIH Bulletin 91: The Rise of the Denim Work Jacket, Z. P. Liollio\nhttps://ticcih.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/TICCIH-Bulletin-91-FINAL.pdf\nIndustrial-heritage research illustrates a circa-1870 work blouse and 1860s portrait; cotton work outerwear predates this 1899 character. Does not authenticate every seam of the generated jacket."},
    {"ringleader", "Ringleader — Fictional composite", "Managing cattle crews required practical work.\nThomas Hooban's 1880-81 ranch ledger records\nworkers' wages, cattle and supplies.", "NPS Grant-Kohrs Ranch: Cowboy Scene Investigation", "https://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/teachers/lessonplans/CSI%20Teachers%20Background.pdf", "1880–1881 · Montana cattle country\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional organizer reflects the practical work of assembling and managing cattle crews. Thomas Hooban’s 1880–81 ranch ledger records workers’ wages, cattle and supplies.\n\nNPS Grant-Kohrs Ranch: Cowboy Scene Investigation\nhttps://www.nps.gov/common/uploads/teachers/lessonplans/CSI%20Teachers%20Background.pdf\nThomas Hooban’s 1880–81 ledger records cattle operations, crew wages and purchases, supporting trail-crew organization and recordkeeping.\n\nNPS: Cowboy Gear\nhttps://www.nps.gov/pisp/learn/photosmultimedia/cowboy-gear.htm\nFunctional felt hats, kerchiefs, leather riding equipment; individual colors and cuts remain reconstruction.\n\nNPS: Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nPeriod sack/frock coats and waistcoats; long high-necked dresses. General clothing context, not this exact costume.\n\nThe Henry Ford: Men’s Suit, circa 1880\nhttps://www.thehenryford.org/collections/explore/artifact/340153\nSurviving wool suit provides additional period tailoring context; generated color and face remain fictional."},
    {"trail-organizer", "Trail Organizer — Fictional composite", "Jenny Fletcher helped build Nicodemus, Kansas.\nShe taught its first school and joined its AME\ncongregation from 1879. This organizer is fictional.", "NPS: Jenny Fletcher", "https://home.nps.gov/people/jenny-fletcher.htm", "Late 1870s–1890s · Kansas frontier community context\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional community organizer reflects a documented role for Black women in the West. Jenny Fletcher helped build Nicodemus, Kansas, teaching its first school and participating in its AME congregation from 1879.\n\nNPS: Jenny Fletcher\nhttps://home.nps.gov/people/jenny-fletcher.htm\n1877 arrival, 1877–78 school, 1879 AME church and community organization. Undated portrait provides high-necked civilian dress context, not exact model clothing.\n\nNPS: Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nPeriod sack/frock coats and waistcoats; long high-necked dresses. General clothing context, not this exact costume."},
    {"blacksmith", "Blacksmith — Fictional composite", "Louis Southworth worked as a blacksmith in\nBuena Vista, Oregon, in 1870. He documents Black\nskilled work; this composite is not his portrait.", "Oregon Historical Society / Oregon Encyclopedia — Louis Southworth (1829–1917)", "https://www.oregonencyclopedia.org/articles/southworth_louis_1829_1917_/", "1870s–1880s · Western United States; documented comparison Buena Vista, Oregon\n\nFull historical note:\nLouis Southworth operated a livery stable and worked as a blacksmith in Buena Vista, Oregon, in 1870. His life documents Black skilled work in Western communities despite Oregon’s exclusion laws; this game character is a fictional composite, not his portrait.\n\nOregon Historical Society / Oregon Encyclopedia — Louis Southworth (1829–1917)\nhttps://www.oregonencyclopedia.org/articles/southworth_louis_1829_1917_/\nDocuments Southworth as a Black Oregon resident and blacksmith in 1870; institutional historical essay with museum portrait references."},
    {"carpenter", "Carpenter — Fictional composite", "An 1874 print shows builders using hand tools.\nFolding measuring rules also predate this era.\nCarpentry required measurement and joinery.", "Library of Congress — Prang’s aid for object teaching: Carpenter, 1874", "https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/98514378/", "1870s–1880s · United States; Western settlement interpretation\n\nFull historical note:\nAn 1874 teaching print in the Library of Congress shows carpenters constructing a building with hand tools. Folding measuring rules also predate the Western setting; carpentry involved measurement and joinery as well as heavy labor.\n\nLibrary of Congress — Prang’s aid for object teaching: Carpenter, 1874\nhttps://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/98514378/\nDated occupational illustration showing building construction and hand-tool work; not a Western portrait.\n\nSmithsonian — Carpenter’s Slide Rule by Bradburn & Son\nhttps://www.si.edu/object/carpenters-slide-rule-bradburn-son%3Anmah_1215015\nMuseum evidence for historic folding carpenter rules; does not authenticate the exact rendered rule."},
    {"sarah-winnemucca", "Sarah Winnemucca — Historical person", "Winnemucca interpreted and advocated for Paiutes.\nAfter forced removal from Malheur to Yakama,\nshe campaigned for their return and rights.", "NPS: Sarah Winnemucca", "https://www.nps.gov/people/sarah-winnemucca.htm", "Late 1870s · Nevada, Oregon and Washington Territory\n\nFull historical note:\nSarah Winnemucca worked as an interpreter and advocate for Northern Paiute people. After the 1878 Bannock War, Paiutes were forcibly removed from Malheur to Yakama; she campaigned for their return and rights.\n\nNPS: Sarah Winnemucca\nhttps://www.nps.gov/people/sarah-winnemucca.htm\nNorthern Paiute identity, interpretation and advocacy; explains that the 1883 lecture costume was a nontraditional stage presentation."},
    {"getaway-rider", "Getaway Rider — Fictional composite", "Mounted contract carriers served postal routes.\nHorseback mail outlasted the Pony Express.\nThis courier and the rescue ability are fictional.", "Smithsonian National Postal Museum: Moving the Mail—Star Routes", "https://postalmuseum.si.edu/moving-the-mail-star-routes", "1880s · Western United States, mounted contract-mail routes\n\nFull historical note:\nFictional mounted courier, drawing on nineteenth-century contract mail carriers. Horseback transport continued on star routes long after the Pony Express ended; the game’s rescue ability is fictional.\n\nSmithsonian National Postal Museum: Moving the Mail—Star Routes\nhttps://postalmuseum.si.edu/moving-the-mail-star-routes\nHorseback and horse-drawn contract transport continued throughout the nineteenth century; role is a mounted courier, not a Pony Express rider in the 1880s.\n\nUSPS Historian: Star Routes, May 2007\nhttps://about.usps.com/who/profile/history/pdf/star-routes.pdf\nAuthoritative account of nineteenth-century mounted contract carriers. Postal context supports mailbag; it does not authenticate a particular fictional rescue or outlaw biography.\n\nLibrary of Congress: A Round Up, 1887\nhttps://www.loc.gov/item/2006686840/\nPeriod Western mounted working-men clothing context: felt hats, shirts, waistcoats, trousers, riding boots and leather tack; muted fabric colors are reconstruction, not measured from monochrome photography."},
    {"prospector", "Prospector — Fictional composite", "Placer miners panned stream deposits for gold.\nAn 1889 photograph records this work at\nRockerville, Dakota Territory.", "Library of Congress: “We have it rich,” Rockerville, Dakota, 1889", "https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/99613951/", "1870s–1880s · Western placer-mining districts; photographic comparison at Rockerville, Dakota Territory\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional prospector uses the pan associated with placer mining. An 1889 photograph from Rockerville, Dakota Territory, records miners washing and panning stream deposits for gold.\n\nLibrary of Congress: “We have it rich,” Rockerville, Dakota, 1889\nhttps://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/99613951/\nDated primary photograph of Spriggs, Lamb and Dillon washing and panning gold; clothing and pan equipment context."},
    {"assayer", "Assayer — Fictional composite", "Assayers measured precious metals in mine samples.\nDenver had a U.S. assay office by 1870.\nThis linked mining with bullion valuation.", "Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis / U.S. Mint — Annual Report, 1870", "https://fraser.stlouisfed.org/files/docs/publications/usmint/usmint_1870_annual-report.pdf", "1870s–1880s · Western United States mining towns\n\nFull historical note:\nAssayers determined the precious-metal content of material brought from mines. The 1870 U.S. Mint report already records Denver operating as an assay office, connecting mining districts with bullion valuation.\n\nFederal Reserve Bank of St. Louis / U.S. Mint — Annual Report, 1870\nhttps://fraser.stlouisfed.org/files/docs/publications/usmint/usmint_1870_annual-report.pdf\nContemporary report identifies Denver as an assay office and describes melting, assaying and stamping bullion; indexed text reviewed.\n\nIdaho State Historical Society — U.S. Assay Office nomination\nhttps://history.idaho.gov/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/US_Assay_Office_66000305.pdf\nBoise assay-office building dated 1870–71; institutional evidence for occupation and location, not clothing.\n\nNational Park Service — Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nPeriod menswear used layered suit components including waistcoats; this supports the garment category, not this work outfit."},
    {"rustler", "Rustler — Fictional composite", "Disputes over unbranded cattle fueled conflict\nin 1880s-1890s Wyoming. An accusation of rustling\ndid not prove theft. This cattle thief is fictional.", "Wyoming Historical Society: The Johnson County War", "https://wyohistory.org/encyclopedia/johnson-county-war-1892-invasion-northern-wyoming", "1880s–1890s · Wyoming range country\n\nFull historical note:\nThis is a fictional cattle thief. In 1880s–1890s Wyoming, disputes over unbranded cattle and accusations of rustling helped drive conflict; an accusation did not itself prove theft.\n\nWyoming Historical Society: The Johnson County War\nhttps://wyohistory.org/encyclopedia/johnson-county-war-1892-invasion-northern-wyoming\nContested rustling accusations and power struggles among large cattlemen and smaller operators.\n\nNPS: Cowboy Gear\nhttps://www.nps.gov/pisp/learn/photosmultimedia/cowboy-gear.htm\nFunctional felt hats, kerchiefs, leather riding equipment; individual colors and cuts remain reconstruction."},
    {"drover", "Drover — Fictional composite", "Black cowboys worked cattle after emancipation.\nNat Love and Bose Ikard rode Western trails.\nThis drover is a fictional composite.", "NPS: Engaging with Cowpoke History", "https://home.nps.gov/articles/000/engaging-with-cowpoke-history.htm", "1870s–1880s · Texas and western cattle trails\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional drover reflects the Black cowboys who worked western cattle herds after emancipation. Nat Love and Bose Ikard are documented examples of men who rode cattle trails and worked with ranching outfits.\n\nNPS: Engaging with Cowpoke History\nhttps://home.nps.gov/articles/000/engaging-with-cowpoke-history.htm\nBlack cowboys, including Nat Love and Bose Ikard, and cattle work.\n\nLibrary of Congress: A Round Up, 1887\nhttps://www.loc.gov/item/2006686840/\nPeriod mounted working-men clothing and riding equipment context."},
    {"annie-oakley", "Annie Oakley — Historical person", "Oakley joined Buffalo Bill's Wild West in 1885.\nHer marksmanship was a professional exhibition.\nStage dress was not everyday frontier clothing.", "Smithsonian National Postal Museum — A Sure Shot: Annie Oakley", "https://postalmuseum.si.edu/exhibition/women-on-stamps-part-4-theatre-and-dance/a-sure-shot-annie-oakley", "late 1880s · United States; touring Wild West performances\n\nFull historical note:\nAnnie Oakley joined Buffalo Bill’s Wild West in 1885. Her marksmanship was a professional exhibition skill; a performance costume should not be treated as the everyday dress of all frontier women.\n\nSmithsonian National Postal Museum — A Sure Shot: Annie Oakley\nhttps://postalmuseum.si.edu/exhibition/women-on-stamps-part-4-theatre-and-dance/a-sure-shot-annie-oakley\nOakley’s identity and Wild West career beginning in 1885; indexed institutional text available, direct page blocked.\n\nSmithsonian National Portrait Gallery — Annie Oakley, John Wood, c.1887\nhttps://npg.si.edu/object/npg_NPG.2007.190\nCatalog dates a portrait to about 1887; birth in 1860. Metadata supports dating, not the mesh’s exact green fabric or firearm."},
    {"sharpshooter", "Sharpshooter — Fictional composite", "Sharps rifles served hunters and target shooters.\nThe 1879 catalog offered open, globe and vernier\niron sights. This marksman is fictional.", "Smithsonian: Sharps Model 1874 Falling Block Centerfire Sporting Rifle", "https://www.americanhistory.si.edu/collections/object/nmah_1253086", "Late 1870s–1880s · Western United States\n\nFull historical note:\nSharps sporting rifles were used for hunting and competitive shooting in the nineteenth century. The company’s 1879 catalog offered several iron-sight options, including open, globe and vernier sights. This marksman is fictional; his rifle is an interpretation of that documented equipment.\n\nSmithsonian: Sharps Model 1874 Falling Block Centerfire Sporting Rifle\nhttps://www.americanhistory.si.edu/collections/object/nmah_1253086\nSurviving rifle dated circa 1875, with an overall length of 115.57 cm. Supports rifle type and scale, not ownership by this fictional character.\n\nCody Firearms Museum: The Sharps Legacy\nhttps://centerofthewest.org/2016/07/14/sharps/\nMuseum discussion of Sharps sporting rifles and the 1879 catalog’s open, globe, vernier and peep sights. Supports period sight options; it does not establish the rejected short brass telescope.\n\nNational Park Service: Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nGeneral late nineteenth-century male coats, waistcoats and trousers; specific person, color palette and ensemble are reconstructed."},
    {"duelist", "Duelist — Fictional composite", "Hickok fought Davis Tutt in Springfield in 1865.\nFormal quick-draw showdowns were rare.\nLater fiction made them seem more typical.", "Yale: On gunfights, U.S. colonialism, and studying the American West", "https://news.yale.edu/2015/07/21/gunfights-us-colonialism-and-studying-american-west-east-coast", "1865–1880s · Western United States; documented comparison at Springfield, Missouri\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional gunman draws on rare documented confrontations such as Hickok’s 1865 fight with Davis Tutt in Springfield, Missouri. Western fiction later made the formal quick-draw showdown seem more typical than the evidence supports.\n\nYale: On gunfights, U.S. colonialism, and studying the American West\nhttps://news.yale.edu/2015/07/21/gunfights-us-colonialism-and-studying-american-west-east-coast\nHistorian’s discussion of the 1865 confrontation and its later mythology."},
    {"wild-bill-hickok", "Wild Bill Hickok — Historical person", "Hickok served as a Kansas lawman and died in\nDeadwood in 1876. Performance and storytelling\ngrew his legend beyond eyewitness history.", "Kansas Historical Society: James B. Wild Bill Hickok", "https://www.kansashistory.gov/kansapedia/james-b-wild-bill-hickok/16518", "1873–1876 · Kansas / Deadwood, Dakota Territory lifetime context\n\nFull historical note:\nWild Bill Hickok served as a lawman in Kansas cattle towns before his death in Deadwood in 1876. His reputation grew through performance and popular storytelling, so later legends should not be treated as eyewitness history.\n\nKansas Historical Society: James B. Wild Bill Hickok\nhttps://www.kansashistory.gov/kansapedia/james-b-wild-bill-hickok/16518\nKansas lawman career and death at Deadwood in 1876.\n\nKansas Historical Society: James Butler Wild Bill Hickok portrait\nhttps://www.kansashistory.gov/archives/474207\nArchival likeness, long hair and moustache; born 1837, died 1876. Does not establish an exact buckskin outfit or holster configuration."},
    {"vigilante", "Vigilante — Fictional composite", "Montana vigilantes acted outside ordinary courts.\nPunishments included banishment and killing.\nTheir claimed motives remain debated.", "Montana Historical Society: Vigilantes and Vigilantism", "https://mhs.mt.gov/education/StoriesOfTheLand/Part2/Chapter6/Ch6Educators/vigilantes", "1880s · Montana Territory\n\nFull historical note:\nThis fictional vigilante represents extralegal frontier organization. Montana vigilante committees acted outside ordinary courts, with punishment that included banishment and killing; their claimed motives remain debated.\n\nMontana Historical Society: Vigilantes and Vigilantism\nhttps://mhs.mt.gov/education/StoriesOfTheLand/Part2/Chapter6/Ch6Educators/vigilantes\nSelf-appointed groups used extralegal trials, killings and banishment; motives and legitimacy remain contested.\n\nNPS: Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nPeriod sack/frock coats and waistcoats; long high-necked dresses. General clothing context, not this exact costume.\n\nThe Henry Ford: Men’s Suit, circa 1880\nhttps://www.thehenryford.org/collections/explore/artifact/340153\nPeriod civilian wool suiting context; not a vigilante uniform or exact replica."},
    {"town-avenger", "Town Avenger — Fictional composite", "This avenger is fictional, not a documented hero.\nVigilantes punished people outside courts.\nTheir acts could include coercion and murder.", "Montana Historical Society: Vigilantes and Vigilantism", "https://mhs.mt.gov/education/StoriesOfTheLand/Part2/Chapter6/Ch6Educators/vigilantes", "1880s · Montana Territory\n\nFull historical note:\nThis is a fictional avenger, not a documented hero. Western vigilante groups sometimes imposed punishments outside courts; their actions could include coercion and murder as well as claims of protecting communities.\n\nMontana Historical Society: Vigilantes and Vigilantism\nhttps://mhs.mt.gov/education/StoriesOfTheLand/Part2/Chapter6/Ch6Educators/vigilantes\nSelf-appointed groups used extralegal trials, killings and banishment; motives and legitimacy remain contested.\n\nNPS: Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nPeriod sack/frock coats and waistcoats; long high-necked dresses. General clothing context, not this exact costume.\n\nLACMA: Routzahn & Gilkey, Man’s Suit, 1885–1895\nhttps://collections.lacma.org/object/4243\nSurviving Los Angeles wool frock coat, waistcoat and trousers; comparative construction context, not an exact color or tailoring copy."},
    {"civil-war-veteran", "Civil War Veteran — Fictional composite", "Union veterans founded the Grand Army of the\nRepublic in 1866 to support fellow veterans.\nA blue coat alone cannot prove wartime service.", "Library of Congress — Grand Army of the Republic and Kindred Societies", "https://guides.loc.gov/grand-army-of-the-republic", "1870s–1880s · Postwar United States; Western civilian interpretation\n\nFull historical note:\nUnion veterans founded the Grand Army of the Republic in 1866. The organization supported veterans and preserved Union memory after the war; membership or particular wartime service cannot be inferred from a blue coat alone.\n\nLibrary of Congress — Grand Army of the Republic and Kindred Societies\nhttps://guides.loc.gov/grand-army-of-the-republic\nFounding of first GAR post in Decatur on April 6, 1866, and nature of veterans’ societies.\n\nNational Park Service — Reconstruction Era\nhttps://www.nps.gov/articles/000/reconstruction-era.htm\nGAR advocacy for benefits and Union memory."},
    {"buffalo-soldier-veteran", "Buffalo Soldier Veteran — Fictional composite", "Black Army regiments were established in 1866.\nThey guarded travel routes at Western posts\nand enforced U.S. expansion against Native peoples.", "National Park Service, Fort Davis — Buffalo Soldiers", "https://home.nps.gov/foda/learn/historyculture/buffalo-soldiers.htm", "1880s–1890s · Western United States; Fort Davis as documented comparison\n\nFull historical note:\nThe 1866 Army reorganization created Black cavalry and infantry regiments. These soldiers served at Western posts including Fort Davis; their work included protecting travel routes and enforcing U.S. expansion against Native peoples.\n\nNational Park Service, Fort Davis — Buffalo Soldiers\nhttps://home.nps.gov/foda/learn/historyculture/buffalo-soldiers.htm\n1866 organization, Western service and period unit photographs around 1875."},
    {"wyatt-earp", "Wyatt Earp — Historical person", "Wyatt joined Virgil, Morgan and Doc Holliday in\nTombstone's October 26, 1881 gunfight.\nVirgil was marshal; Wyatt was not sole leader.", "NPS: Virgil Earp", "https://www.nps.gov/home/learn/historyculture/virgil-earp.htm", "1881 · Tombstone, Arizona Territory\n\nFull historical note:\nWyatt Earp took part in Tombstone’s October 26, 1881 gunfight alongside Virgil and Morgan Earp and Doc Holliday. Virgil was the town marshal; Wyatt’s role should not be inflated into sole lawman leadership.\n\nNPS: Virgil Earp\nhttps://www.nps.gov/home/learn/historyculture/virgil-earp.htm\nTombstone arrival in 1879, Virgil as marshal and brothers as part-time deputies; use only these supported facts.\n\nArizona State Library: Gunfight at the O.K. Corral\nhttps://azmemory.azlibrary.gov/nodes/view/164513\nOctober 26, 1881 event and participants, archival context.\n\nNPS: Gilded Age Fashion\nhttps://home.nps.gov/vama/blogs/gilded-age-fashion.htm\nPeriod sack/frock coats and waistcoats; long high-necked dresses. General clothing context, not this exact costume."},
    {"quanah-parker", "Quanah Parker — Historical person", "Quanah brought his Comanche followers to Fort Sill\nin 1875. He later negotiated reservation life\nand developed cattle-ranching interests.", "NPS: Quanah Parker", "https://www.nps.gov/people/quanah-parker.htm", "1870s; later biography identified separately · Comanche homelands and Fort Sill, Indian Territory\n\nFull historical note:\nQuanah Parker brought his remaining Comanche followers to Fort Sill in 1875. He later negotiated reservation life and developed cattle-ranching interests; his career spans resistance and adaptation.\n\nNPS: Quanah Parker\nhttps://www.nps.gov/people/quanah-parker.htm\nComanche identity, 1875 surrender and subsequent ranching/leadership.\n\nSmithsonian National Portrait Gallery: Quanah Parker, 1890\nhttps://npg.si.edu/object/npg_NPG.96.178\nA dated later portrait documents likeness context; it does not prove the model’s earlier red-shirt ensemble."},
    {"augustus-powder-pike", "Augustus “Powder” Pike — Fictional composite", "Alfred Nobel patented dynamite in 1867.\nIts manufacture expanded in the following years.\nPowder Pike is a fictional blasting worker.", "Norwegian Nobel Committee — Alfred Nobel", "https://www.nobelpeaceprize.org/nobel-peace-prize/history/alfred-nobel", "1870s–1890s · Western mining districts\n\nFull historical note:\nAlfred Nobel patented dynamite in 1867, and its manufacture expanded during the following years. “Powder Pike” is fictional; this context explains an industrial blasting role, not a historical individual.\n\nNorwegian Nobel Committee — Alfred Nobel\nhttps://www.nobelpeaceprize.org/nobel-peace-prize/history/alfred-nobel\nDynamite patent in 1867.\n\nNobel Prize — The Nobel Dynamite Companies\nhttps://www.nobelprize.org/alfred-nobel/the-nobel-dynamite-companies/\nIndustrial manufacture and expansion of dynamite; does not document body-worn explosive bundles."},
    {"cornelius-ashby", "Cornelius Ashby, Railroad Baron — Fictional composite", "Ashby is fictional. Executives such as Stanford\ndirected rail expansion. Southern Pacific's\nsouthern transcontinental line was complete in 1883.", "NPS: The Southern Transcontinental Railroad", "https://home.nps.gov/amis/learn/historyculture/str.htm", "1870s–1880s · Western United States railroad business\n\nFull historical note:\nCornelius Ashby is fictional. Railroad executives such as Leland Stanford helped finance and direct western rail expansion; the Southern Pacific completed a southern transcontinental route in 1883.\n\nNPS: The Southern Transcontinental Railroad\nhttps://home.nps.gov/amis/learn/historyculture/str.htm\nThe Big Four’s role and the 1883 southern transcontinental connection.\n\nMcCord Stewart Museum: Cutaway coat\nhttps://collections.musee-mccord-stewart.ca/en/objects/37938/no-title\nMuseum collection documents late-nineteenth-century formal coat tailoring; broad clothing context, not Ashby’s invented outfit."},
    {"charles-goodnight", "Charles Goodnight — Historical person", "Goodnight and Loving drove cattle from Texas\ntoward Fort Sumner in 1866. Goodnight later\nestablished ranches in the Texas Panhandle.", "Texas Historical Commission — Goodnight Ranch History", "https://thc.texas.gov/state-historic-sites/goodnight-ranch/goodnight-ranch-history", "circa 1880 · Texas Panhandle\n\nFull historical note:\nCharles Goodnight and Oliver Loving drove cattle from Texas toward Fort Sumner in 1866. Goodnight later established ranching operations in the Texas Panhandle and helped organize the Panhandle Stockmen’s Association in 1880.\n\nTexas Historical Commission — Goodnight Ranch History\nhttps://thc.texas.gov/state-historic-sites/goodnight-ranch/goodnight-ranch-history\n1866 drive, Panhandle ranching and 1880 association. Detailed page distinguishes move in 1876 from Adair partnership in 1877; avoid the inconsistent shorthand elsewhere on the site."},
    {"poker-alice", "Poker Alice — Historical person", "Alice Ivers Tubbs made her living by gambling\nin Western towns. Her career led from Colorado\nmining communities to Deadwood by 1892.", "Denver Public Library: “Poker” Alice Tubbs (1851–1930)", "https://history.denverlibrary.org/colorado-biographies/poker-alice-tubbs-1851-1930", "Circa 1890 · Colorado mining towns and, by 1892, Deadwood, South Dakota\n\nFull historical note:\nAlice Ivers Tubbs, known as Poker Alice, made a living at gambling tables in western towns. Denver Public Library traces her career from Colorado mining communities to Deadwood, where she had settled by 1892.\n\nDenver Public Library: “Poker” Alice Tubbs (1851–1930)\nhttps://history.denverlibrary.org/colorado-biographies/poker-alice-tubbs-1851-1930\nBiographical dates, gambling career, 1892 Deadwood residence and explicit caution that much of her story comes from her own account."},
